"The twelve main meridians introduced earlier in this unit each belong to a specific organ, run through a specific limb, and follow a fixed daily sequence. Chinese medicine describes eight additional channels that don't follow any of those rules, called the \"eight extraordinary vessels.\" They aren't matched to individual organs, and they don't carry qi in the same continuous, hand-to-organ relay described earlier — instead, they're traditionally compared to reservoirs, holding and releasing qi and blood to support the twelve regular meridians when needed, rather than being part of the everyday flow themselves.": "ad0ea32f7756",
"The twelve main meridians introduced earlier in this unit each belong to a specific organ, run through a specific limb, and follow a fixed daily sequence. Chinese medicine describes eight additional channels that don't follow any of those rules, called the \"eight extraordinary vessels.\" They aren't matched to individual organs, and they don't carry qi in the same continuous, hand-to-organ relay described earlier — instead, they're traditionally compared to reservoirs, holding and releasing qi and blood to support the twelve regular meridians when needed, rather than being part of the everyday flow themselves. Two of the eight carry particular weight because, unlike the other six, they have their own dedicated acupuncture points rather than only borrowing points from the twelve regular meridians. The Governing Vessel runs up the center of the back and is associated with all of the body's yang meridians, sometimes described as the \"sea of yang.\" The Conception Vessel runs up the center of the front of the body and is associated with all of the yin meridians, sometimes described as the \"sea of yin\" — together forming a kind of central spine for the entire meridian system. The reservoir comparison matters because it explains why these eight vessels come up specifically in discussions of chronic, long-standing conditions rather than sudden ones. A sudden problem is usually read through the twelve regular meridians, the way a delayed bus is read through the regular schedule rather than the emergency backup system. The eight extraordinary vessels are the backup system — rarely the first place a doctor looks, but the reserve that becomes relevant when the everyday network has been under strain for a long time.": "575262777cba",
